title: 'Global Time Echoes IV: Held-Out Replication in the Public MGEX Combined Multi-GNSS
  Clock Product, 2025–2026'
abstract: The Temporal Equivalence Principle (TEP) predicts correlated phase-coherent
  disturbances in GNSS timekeeping with a spatial correlation length of order thousands
  of kilometres, an east–west anisotropy exceeding the north–south counterpart, coupling
  to Earth orbital velocity, and a preferred axis near the CMB rest frame. Papers
  1–3 established these signatures in GPS-only precise point positioning (PPP) products
  and raw RINEX observations. This paper presents a strict held-out replication test
  using the public MGEX combined multi-GNSS receiver-clock product (CODE COD0MGXFIN)
  from NASA CDDIS / IGS for 2025–2026. The product provides a single combined receiver-clock
  solution per station; no positioning is performed and no raw RINEX files are used.
  All predictions—correlation length, azimuthal anisotropy, orbital-velocity coupling,
  CMB-frame alignment, ionospheric independence, and geometric robustness—were frozen
  before the held-out data were inspected. Four of five evaluated signatures are recovered,
  strengthening the case against epoch-specific and GPS-PPP-specific systematics.
